B.append(['title','Cryptanalysis of the Uzbek Hash Function Standard O‘zMSt 285:2024 and a Faster Redesign with Provable Differential Bounds'])
B.append(['sub','[Author Name 1], [Author Name 2], and [Author Name 3]'])
P("[Affiliations]. Corresponding author: [Name] ([e-mail]).")
P(f"*Abstract*—O‘zMSt 285:2024 is the national hash function standard of Uzbekistan. Its main algorithm processes a 4×8 state through ten rounds built from modular arithmetic and a parameterized inversion-based mixing step, with message injection only at three absorption points per block. We present the first public analysis of its 256-bit and 128-bit modes against linear, differential, algebraic and integral cryptanalysis. We show that all non-absorbing rounds are T-functions: the least significant bit layer evolves through an exact linear map, most-significant-bit differences survive each round with probability about 0.9, and integral properties persist for seven rounds. In the 128-bit mode this leads to practical attacks on the full hash: a 32-bit output layer is an affine function of the message, recoverable from 80 queries; a 32-bit integral distinguisher; and collisions with complexity 2^48. The mixing matrix is singular modulo 2 for {100-full:.0f}% of keys, yielding constant output relations and internal collisions. We then propose and evaluate a redesign: an AES S-box based absorption, odd round keys, a new bijective mixing step called Bog‘lash, a state-independent key schedule, feed-forward with an output transformation, and a unified byte-oriented 128-bit mode. We prove that each Bog‘lash call has at least eight active S-boxes, so every round contributes at least 16 and differential trails over one round have probability at most 2^-96; linear trails reach 2^-64 after three rounds in our model. With six rounds the redesign is 1.8–3.6 times faster than the standard in C.")
P("*Index Terms*—Hash functions, national cryptographic standards, linear cryptanalysis, differential cryptanalysis, algebraic cryptanalysis, integral cryptanalysis, T-functions, S-boxes, active S-boxes, O‘zMSt 285:2024.")

H1('I. Introduction')
P("Cryptographic hash functions underpin digital signatures, message authentication, key derivation and the integrity of software and data [@shs], [@sha3]. In addition to the internationally standardized SHA-2 and SHA-3 families, several countries maintain national hash standards, such as GOST R 34.11-2012 (Streebog) in Russia [@gost], Kupyna (DSTU 7564:2014) in Ukraine [@kupyna] and SM3 in China [@sm3]. National designs are typically adopted for sovereignty and regulatory reasons, and their long-term trustworthiness depends on open, independent cryptanalysis: the history of MD5 and SHA-1 [@wang] shows that weaknesses of widely deployed hash functions were found only after sustained public scrutiny. The same need is reflected in the growing number of design-and-analysis studies of new hash functions published in recent years [@windarta22], [@windarta23], [@nabeel21], [@sakan25].")
P("O‘zMSt 285:2024 is the current hash function standard of Uzbekistan [@std]. Its first algorithm (Algorithm 1) supports a 256-bit mode operating on bytes and a 128-bit mode operating on 4-bit words, with an optional key. The design differs considerably from mainstream constructions: it uses no S-box in the round function; instead, it relies on a parameterized modular inversion called *Qo‘sh* whose parameters depend on sums and products of two arrays, a modular matrix multiplication *Aralash*, cyclic shifts, and a byte compression *BaytZichlash* applied only at three message absorption points per block. To the best of our knowledge, no public cryptanalysis of this standard has been published.")
P("This paper makes the following contributions.")
BL("We give a precise description of Algorithm 1, including implementation conventions that are ambiguous in the standard text and that we resolved by reproducing the official test vectors byte by byte.")
BL("We show that all non-absorbing rounds are T-functions [@klimov] and derive the consequences for four classes of attacks: an exact linear relation on the least significant bit (LSB) layer, high-probability most significant bit (MSB) differential trails, degree-one equations on the LSB layer, and seven-round integral distinguishers.")
BL("For the 128-bit mode we obtain practical attacks on the full hash function: prediction of 32 output bits from 80 queries with 100% accuracy, separate recovery of 30 key bits, a 32-bit integral distinguisher and collisions with complexity 2^48. For both modes we show that the mixing matrix is singular modulo 2 for almost all keys, giving constant output relations and internal collisions.")
BL("We propose a redesign consisting of four recommendations and a new mixing step, *Bog‘lash*, prove lower bounds on the number of active S-boxes, and report statistical tests and C benchmarks: the redesign removes all identified weaknesses and is 1.8–3.6 times faster than the standard with six rounds.")
P("The paper is organized as follows. Section II reviews related work and Section III introduces notation. Section IV describes Algorithm 1 and Section V presents its cryptanalysis. Section VI describes the redesign, Section VII evaluates its security and Section VIII its performance. Section IX discusses limitations and Section X concludes.")

H1('II. Related Work')
H2('A. National and Recent Hash Function Designs')
P("Most recent hash standards follow one of two paths. SHA-2 and SM3 iterate a compression function with a large number of simple steps in the Merkle–Damgård mode [@shs], [@sm3], whereas SHA-3 uses a sponge construction over a 1600-bit permutation [@sha3]. Streebog and Kupyna compress with AES-like permutations or block ciphers combined with a feed-forward or a Grøstl-like output transformation [@gost], [@kupyna], [@grostl]. Table @T:std compares their structural parameters with O‘zMSt 285:2024. A common feature of all modern designs is a nonlinear substitution layer with well-understood differential and linear properties, which O‘zMSt 285:2024 lacks inside its rounds.")
T([2000,1450,1300,1350,1700,1560],[["Hash function","Construction","Block (bits)","Output (bits)","Rounds / steps","Nonlinear layer"],["SHA-256 [@shs]","Merkle–Damgård","512","256","64 steps","Boolean functions, ARX"],["SHA3-256 [@sha3]","sponge","1088 (rate)","256","24 rounds","χ (degree 2)"],["SM3 [@sm3]","Merkle–Damgård","512","256","64 steps","Boolean functions, ARX"],["Streebog [@gost]","MD with feed-forward","512","256/512","12 rounds","8-bit S-box"],["Kupyna-256 [@kupyna]","Grøstl-like","512","256","10 rounds","four 8-bit S-boxes"],["O‘zMSt 285:2024 [@std]","iterated, 3 absorptions/block","256 / 128","256 / 128","10 rounds/block","Qo‘sh (modular inversion), Z only at absorption"]],"Structural comparison of hash function standards","std")
P("Recent IEEE Access articles have proposed and analysed lightweight hash functions for constrained devices. Windarta *et al.* surveyed lightweight hash designs and their cryptanalysis [@windarta22] and later proposed two Saturnin-based hash functions evaluated by differential and linear analysis and statistical tests [@windarta23]; Nabeel *et al.* analysed the security of a Mersenne-number-based hash function for the Internet of Things [@nabeel21]; Thakor *et al.* reviewed lightweight primitives for resource-constrained devices [@thakor21]. In Central Asia, Sakan *et al.* designed a lightweight hash function and evaluated it with SAT-based algebraic cryptanalysis [@sakan25]. Our work differs in that it analyses an adopted national standard and evaluates a redesign with active S-box bounds.")
H2('B. S-Boxes and Automated Bounds')
P("The resistance of substitution–permutation designs is usually argued by counting active S-boxes, following the wide-trail strategy [@rijndael] and the theory of differentially uniform mappings [@nyberg]. Many IEEE Access papers construct new S-boxes, for example from Gaussian distributions [@khan19] or by heuristic evolution [@zahid21]; Dimitrov critically examined the design of chaos-based S-boxes [@dimitrov20], and Acikkapi *et al.* studied their side-channel behaviour [@acikkapi19]. We deliberately use the AES S-box [@aes], whose differential uniformity 4 and nonlinearity 112 are optimal among known 8-bit permutations and well studied. Minimal numbers of active S-boxes are commonly computed with mixed-integer linear programming [@mouha], as in the MILP-based differential cryptanalysis of Midori64 [@zhao20] and the active S-box analysis of a recent SPN–ARX block cipher [@alawida26]; Idris *et al.* predicted active S-box counts with deep learning [@idris21]. We obtain our differential bound by an exact dynamic program over truncated patterns, and identify an MILP confirmation of the linear bound as future work.")
H2('C. T-Functions and Classical Attacks')
P("T-functions were introduced by Klimov and Shamir as a class of invertible mappings built from modular arithmetic [@klimov]; their single-bit triangular structure is attractive for efficient designs but must be combined with operations that move information from high to low bits. Our analysis uses linear [@matsui], differential [@biham], algebraic [@courtois], [@cube] and integral (square) [@square], [@integral] cryptanalysis, and the classification of block-cipher-based compression functions [@pgv], [@brs] for the feed-forward design.")

H1('III. Preliminaries')
P("For a map F on n-bit words, the correlation of a linear approximation with input mask α and output mask β is")
F("c_F(α → β) = 2⁻ⁿ · Σₓ (−1)^(β·F(x) ⊕ α·x),")
P("and a linear attack requires about c⁻² data [@matsui]. The difference distribution table entry DDT_F(Δx, Δy) is the number of inputs x with F(x) ⊕ F(x ⊕ Δx) = Δy, and a differential characteristic has probability equal to the product of its transition probabilities [@biham]. For an S-box with maximal differential probability p and maximal absolute correlation c, any characteristic (linear trail) with N active S-boxes has probability at most pᴺ (correlation at most cᴺ) [@rijndael], [@nyberg]. For the AES S-box, p = 2⁻⁶ and c = 2⁻³ [@rijndael].")
P("A map on n-bit words is a T-function if bit j of its output depends only on bits 0, …, j of its inputs [@klimov]. Addition, subtraction, multiplication and inversion modulo 2ⁿ are T-functions. In integral cryptanalysis [@square], [@integral] a set of inputs is chosen such that some words take all values (A) and others are constant (C); a word whose XOR-sum over the set is zero is balanced (B). Table @T:not lists our notation.")
T([2300,7060],[["Symbol","Meaning"],["holat, kₑ, k","state array, round-key array, key (4×8 elements)"],["p","modulus: 256 (256-bit mode) or 16 (128-bit mode)"],["[s,u]","element in row s, column u"],["h₀[s,u], LSB layer","least significant bit of an element; the 32 LSBs of an array"],["M(k₀), P, L","GF(2) matrix of Aralash on the LSB layer, SurHolat permutation, L = P·M(k₀)"],["Δ, ⊕, ∨","XOR difference, bitwise XOR, bitwise OR"],["S","AES S-box [@aes]"],["R","first key element (even); round count of the redesign"],["RCⱼ, C₁, C₂","round constants (√2) and output constants (√3) of the redesign"]],"Notation","not")

H1('IV. Description of Algorithm 1')
H2('A. State, Modes and Block Processing')
P("Algorithm 1 operates on a state array *holat* and a round-key array kₑ, both of size 4×8. In the 256-bit mode the elements are bytes (arithmetic modulo p = 256); in the 128-bit mode they are 4-bit words (p = 16). The message is split into 256-bit (respectively 128-bit) blocks M₁, …, M_b; a length block (the message length in bits) and a sum block NY, equal to M₁ + … + M_b modulo 2 raised to the block length in bits, are appended. The first block becomes the initial state, and each remaining block is processed by one outer iteration of ten rounds. The key k (all-zero in the unkeyed mode) initializes kₑ; its first element R must be even.")
H2('B. Transformations')
P("*Qo‘sh(X, Y)* transforms two arrays jointly. For every element x of X, with y the element of Y at the same position, three steps compute x⁻¹, x⁻² and x⁻³ through")
F("x⁽ᵗ⁾ = −A_t · x⁽ᵗ⁻¹⁾ · (B_t + R · x⁽ᵗ⁻¹⁾)⁻¹ (mod p),   t = 1, 2, 3,")
pm=MR['L1']['param']; a2=MR['A2']; xx=a2['x']
P(f"where A₁ = J₁(Y) + 2y + Δ, B₁ = K₁(X) − 2x, J is the sum of an array, K is the product of its elements with the LSB forced to one, and Δ, Δ′ are corrections that make the parameters odd or avoid specific equalities (a value x⁻² = 0 is replaced by A₂ + B₂). The same transformation is applied to Y with the roles exchanged. Table @T:qosh shows the computation for the first element of the Annex A example. *Aralash(holat, kₑ)* multiplies the left and right 4×4 halves of the state by those of kₑ with a “diamatrix” rule modulo p. *SurHolat* and *SurKalit* are cyclic row and column shifts, and *TuzilmaKalit* adjusts kₑ so that its LSB layer equals that of k. *BaytZichlash* absorbs a block: in the 256-bit mode the new byte is Z(h) ‖ Z(n), where Z is an 8-to-4-bit table and h, n are the state and block bytes after Qo‘sh; in the 128-bit mode it is h ⊕ n.")
T([2300,2600,2400,2060],[["Quantity","Value","Quantity","Value"],["J₁(holat), J₁(block)",f"{a2['J1h']}, {a2['J1hn']}","K₁(holat)",str(a2['K1h'])],["R",str(pm['R']),"A₁, B₁",f"{pm['A1']}, {pm['B1']}"],["A₂, B₂",f"{pm['A2']}, {pm['B2']}","x = holat[0,0]",f"0x{xx[0]:02x}"],["x⁻¹",f"0x{xx[1]:02x}","x⁻², x⁻³",f"0x{xx[2]:02x}, 0x{xx[3]:02x}"]],"Qo‘sh applied to holat[0,0] of the Annex A example (256-bit mode)","qosh")
H2('C. Round Structure')
P("One outer iteration consists of an absorption (Qo‘sh(holat, block), BaytZichlash) followed by ten rounds. Each round applies Aralash(holat, kₑ), Qo‘sh(holat, kₑ), SurHolat and SurKalit, a second Qo‘sh(holat, kₑ) and TuzilmaKalit; in rounds 8 and 9 the second Qo‘sh is replaced or followed by another absorption. Thus a block is absorbed at three points (0, 8 and 9), and rounds 1–7 and 10 are non-absorbing.")
H2('D. Implementation Conventions')
P("The standard text leaves several details ambiguous. By reproducing the test vectors of Annex A byte by byte we established that R is the first key element; arrays are filled row by row; the length block encodes bits in big-endian order; NY is the modular sum of the blocks; and the number of rounds is (b + 1)·10 for b + 1 processed blocks (the standard states (b + 2)·10). We also found misprints in Annex A (e.g., “sc” instead of “cc”) and in Annex B (seven values written in decimal). Our Python and C implementations reproduce all official test vectors; the 256-bit and 128-bit hashes of the Annex A message are listed in Table @T:tv.")

H1('V. Cryptanalysis of the Standard')
H2('A. Structural Weakness: Rounds Are T-Functions')
P("All transformations of a non-absorbing round use only modular addition, multiplication, inversion and position permutations, so each such round is a T-function on the state. Since A and B in Qo‘sh are odd and R is even, the denominator B + R·x is odd and x⁻¹ ≡ x (mod 2); the corrections and the zero replacement add even values. Aralash is linear over ℤ/pℤ for fixed kₑ, hence linear over GF(2) on the LSB layer with a matrix M(k₀) that depends only on the LSB layer k₀ of the key. This yields the following exact relation.")
P("*Lemma 1.* For every non-absorbing round j, the LSB layers of the state satisfy")
F("h₀⁽ʲ⁾ = P · M(k₀) · h₀⁽ʲ⁻¹⁾ = L · h₀⁽ʲ⁻¹⁾   (over GF(2)),")
P("where P is the permutation matrix of SurHolat. In the 128-bit mode the absorption h ⊕ n is also linear, so h₀⁽ʲ⁾ = L·h₀⁽ʲ⁻¹⁾ ⊕ n₀ holds at absorption points as well, and the LSB layer of the hash is an affine function of the LSB layers of the message blocks.")
P(f"We verified Lemma 1 on the Annex A data: after every transformation of round 1 the predicted LSB layer agreed with the actual one in all 32 positions, and over 1500 random keys the relation held with correlation 1 in rounds 1–7 (Fig. @F:f1a).")
H2('B. Linear Cryptanalysis')
T([2600,2200,2200,2360],[["Component","Standard, 256-bit","Standard, 128-bit","Proposed (SBZ)"],["Absorption: max |c|","0.25","1 (XOR)","2⁻⁶"],["Absorption: max DP","40/256 = 0.156","1","4/256 = 2⁻⁶"],["Single-input difference vanishes","0.156","compensable (1)","never"],["Algebraic degree","6","1","13"],["Avalanche (SAC) mean","0.266","0.125","0.503"],["Input–output dependencies","64/128","16/128","128/128"],["Qo‘sh: LSB / bit 1 correlation","1 / ≥ 0.99","1 / ≥ 0.99","—"]],"Properties of the absorption functions (exhaustive over all 2¹⁶ inputs)","comp")
P(f"Table @T:comp summarizes the components. The table Z has maximal correlation 0.25, e.g. z₀ ⊕ z₂ = x₀ ⊕ x₂ ⊕ x₆ ⊕ x₇ with c = 0.25, and the 128-bit XOR absorption is linear. By Lemma 1 non-absorbing rounds contribute nothing to linear resistance; each active byte at an absorption point costs a factor of at most 2⁻² in the 256-bit mode. Searching the minimal number of active bytes over consecutive absorption layers for 120 random keys, we found that the bound 2⁻⁶⁴ is reached after five to six absorption layers for median keys (about 18–19 rounds, i.e., at least three message blocks), whereas for the worst keys every layer can contain a single active byte. The Annex A key is such a key: the row [0,3] of L has a single non-zero entry, so h₀[0,3] passes all non-absorbing rounds unchanged.")
P(f"Because round 10 follows the last absorption, the LSB layer of the hash lies in the image of L. Over 1000 random keys the rank of L was {fz(mean_rank,1)} on average (range {rmin}–32) and equal to 32 for only {fz(full,1)}% of the keys (Fig. @F:rank); hence the output satisfies on average about six linear relations with correlation 1, and more than half of the keys produce constant output bits. For the Annex A key the rank is 24 and the eight relations, e.g. h₀[0,7] ⊕ h₀[1,1] ⊕ h₀[2,2] ⊕ h₀[3,6] = 0, held for all of 1000 random messages.")
IMG('fig3.png',8.0,5.6,"Distribution of the rank of L over GF(2) for 1000 random keys (red: full rank).","rank")
P("*Attack on the 128-bit mode.* In the keyed 128-bit mode an adversary who queries 80 messages can solve for the affine map o₀ = A·m₀ ⊕ c (32 × 33 unknowns) and then predicts the 32 LSBs of the keyed hash of any new message; in our experiments the prediction was correct for all 32 bits in 100% of 300 test messages. Since A and c depend only on the 30 free LSBs of the key, these key bits can be recovered separately with 2³⁰ trials, reducing exhaustive key search to about 2⁹⁶.")
H2('C. Differential Cryptanalysis')
P("Because x + 2^(n−1) ≡ x ⊕ 2^(n−1) (mod 2ⁿ), an MSB difference creates no carries. In Qo‘sh an MSB difference propagates to an MSB difference with probability at least 254/256 per element, and Aralash spreads MSB differences linearly through M(k₀) with probability 1. In experiments with 3000 pairs, a single MSB difference after the first absorption survived each round of the 256-bit mode with probability 0.85–0.94 and seven rounds with probability 0.34 (Fig. @F:f1b). In 3.1% (256-bit) and 3.3% (128-bit) of the pairs the difference lay in the kernel of M(k₀) and vanished with probability 1 already in round 1, producing internal collisions. At the absorption, the transition 0x96 → 0 of Z has probability 40/256, so a state difference can be cancelled independently of the message difference. An upper bound obtained with the best transition per active byte reaches 2⁻¹²⁸ only after the second absorption point (round 9) of the first iteration.")
P("*Collisions in the 128-bit mode.* Since the LSB layer is affine in the message (Lemma 1), two single-block messages that differ only in the top bit layer always produce hash values with equal LSB layers (verified for 2000 pairs). The remaining 96 output bits behave randomly—higher layers are not separated because the corrections Δ depend on all bits—so a birthday search over such messages yields collisions with complexity about 2⁴⁸.")
IMG('fig1.png',16.5,6.1,"(a) Correlation of the LSB-layer relation h₀⁽ʲ⁾ = L·h₀⁽ʲ⁻¹⁾ per round; (b) probability that a single MSB difference stays an MSB-only difference. Shaded: absorption rounds of the standard.","f1")
FK['f1a']=FK['f1']+'a'; FK['f1b']=FK['f1']+'b'
H2('D. Algebraic Cryptanalysis')
P("Only the absorption raises the algebraic degree: Z has degree 6 and admits 43 cubic but no quadratic implicit equations [@courtois]; the 128-bit XOR has degree 1. Qo‘sh dominates the size of the equation system (about 91% of the variables of a round), but its LSB is linear and the degree of bit j is close to j. Cube tests [@cube] on 16 message bits (Table @T:cube) confirm that in the 256-bit mode a single absorption leaves 84% of the state bits with zero cube sums at dimension 12, and that the LSB layer of the 128-bit mode has degree 1 for any number of rounds; this is exploited by the linearization attack of Section V-B.")
