T([3200,2200,1800,2160],[["Attack on the 128-bit mode","Data / queries","Time","Success (experiment)"],["Prediction of 32 hash LSBs (keyed)","80 chosen messages","negligible","100% (300 messages)"],["Recovery of the key LSB layer","80 messages","2³⁰","—"],["Integral distinguisher on the full hash","16–256 chosen messages","negligible","32/32 bits, all 40 trials"],["Collision search (top-layer differences)","—","≈2⁴⁸","LSB layer equal in 2000/2000 pairs"]],"Practical attacks on the 128-bit mode","att")

H1('VI. Proposed Redesign')
P("Our redesign addresses each source of weakness with a targeted change. We first describe four recommendations that can be applied to the standard individually, and then the complete redesign.")
H2('A. Recommendation 1: S-Box-Based Absorption (SBZ)')
P("We replace BaytZichlash by")
F("h ← S(h ⊕ S(n)),")
P("where S is the AES S-box. In the 128-bit mode adjacent 4-bit words are paired into bytes. The correlation and difference tables of SBZ follow exactly from those of S: c_SBZ((α, β) → γ) = c_S(α → γ)·c_S(β → α) and DDT_SBZ((Δh, Δn), Δy) = Σ_d DDT_S(Δn, d)·DDT_S(Δh ⊕ d, Δy). Hence the maximal correlation is 2⁻⁶ (reached by only 0.04% of mask triples), the maximal differential probability is 2⁻⁶ and is reached only for Δn = 0, a difference in a single input never vanishes, and the degree is 13 (Table @T:comp). Applied alone, SBZ removes all practical attacks on the 128-bit mode and lets the 256-bit mode reach 2⁻⁶⁴ linear resistance after four absorption layers for median keys; the throughput is unchanged.")
H2('B. Recommendation 2: Odd Round Keys')
P("A linear map over ℤ/2ⁿℤ is invertible if and only if it is invertible modulo 2; the condition “determinant different from zero” in the standard should read “determinant odd”. We enumerated all 2¹⁶ parity patterns of a 4×4 key half (Table @T:odd). The unique pattern with the maximal branch number 6 is the all-odd pattern. We therefore set kₑ ← kₑ ∨ 1 before the first round and after every key update. Aralash becomes invertible for every key, the output relations, constant bits and kernel collisions disappear, and the minimal number of active bytes over five absorption layers rises to 43 for every key.")
T([3000,1580,1580,1580,1620],[["Parity patterns of a 4×4 key half","Branch 2","Branch 3","Branch 4","Branch 6"],["Invertible patterns (7568 of 65536)","5532","1955","80","1 (all odd)"]],"Branch numbers of the LSB-layer matrix of Aralash for all invertible key parity patterns","odd")
H2('C. Recommendation 3 and a New Mixing Step: Bog‘lash')
P("*Observation.* For fixed parameters, one step of Qo‘sh is the Möbius transformation x ↦ −A·x·(R·x + B)⁻¹, and the composition of its three steps is again a single Möbius transformation (verified for 200 random parameter sets). Such a map is a T-function, so Qo‘sh cannot remove the structure identified in Section V. Moreover, given A and B the element is recovered as x = −B·y·(A + R·y)⁻¹, so the one-wayness claimed in the standard does not hold, and the zero-replacement rule is not needed for invertibility (A, B odd and R even already make the map bijective) but in fact destroys bijectivity.")
P("We replace every Qo‘sh by *Bog‘lash(holat, X)*, which keeps the ideas of Qo‘sh—parameterized Möbius multiplication, global sums and products, coupling of two arrays—but adds S-boxes and a Feistel structure (Fig. @F:bog). The state is split into a left half U and a right half T (the first and second half of every row, m bytes each). A half-step computes")
F("J = Σ U,   K = Π (U ∨ 1),   aᵢ = 2·S(U[i] ⊕ X_U[i] ⊕ J) + 1,   bᵢ = 2·S(U[i+1] ⊕ X_T[i] ⊕ K) + 1,")
F("T[i] ← S( −aᵢ · T[i] · (bᵢ + R·T[i])⁻¹ ⊕ X_T[i] )   (mod 256),")
P("and Bog‘lash applies three half-steps: right ← f(left), left ← f(right), right ← f(left). Since aᵢ, bᵢ are odd and R is even, every half-step is a bijection of the modified half for fixed X; Bog‘lash is therefore a bijection of the state with an explicit inverse and has no data-dependent branches. Table @T:diff shows why the third half-step is necessary: after two half-steps a byte of the right half influences only 16 bytes, after three it influences 31 of 32 bytes. A Bog‘lash call costs 48 modular inversions, compared with 192 for Qo‘sh (Table @T:cost). Bog‘lash also implements Recommendation 3, which asks for an element that moves information from high to low bits: the S-box makes every output bit depend on all input bits (Fig. @F:sac).")
IMG('fig6.png',16.5,6.6,"Structure of Bog‘lash: three Feistel-like half-steps; parameters of each half-step are derived from the unchanged half through the AES S-box.","bog")
T([3400,1980,1980,2000],[["Half-steps executed","Active left byte → active bytes","Active right byte → active bytes","Comment"],["1","16.5","1.0","left byte reaches the whole right half via J, K"],["2","30.9","16.4",""],["3","31.3","30.9","full diffusion in one call"]],"Diffusion inside one Bog‘lash call (average number of active output bytes, 1500 pairs, 256-bit mode)","diff")
IMG('fig4.png',16.5,7.0,"Strict avalanche matrices of one round: (a) standard — 12.4% of the input–output bit pairs never interact; (b) redesign — all pairs interact, mean 0.500.","sac")
H2('D. Recommendation 4: Feed-Forward and Output Transformation')
P("With a bijective round function and the first message block as initial state, the hash could be computed backwards from its output. A public bijective output layer does not help: applying the public inverse recovers the internal state, and in our experiments the output relations remained after an S-box layer. We therefore add a Davies–Meyer feed-forward [@pgv], [@brs] and an output transformation in the style of Grøstl [@grostl]:")
F("holatᵢ = F(holatᵢ₋₁, Bᵢ, kₑ) ⊕ holatᵢ₋₁,      h(M) = P(H) ⊕ H,   P = Bog‘lash(Bog‘lash(H, C₁), C₂),")
P("where H is the final state and C₁, C₂ are constants. Each part alone is insufficient: the feed-forward removes constant output relations but, being linear, preserves the affine LSB layer of the 128-bit mode, while the output transformation alone removes output-level distinguishers but not backward computation. Together they remove all output-level properties in both modes.")
H2('E. Complete Redesign')
P("The complete redesign (“unified variant”) combines the above. Both modes operate on bytes: the state and the key are 4×8 bytes in the 256-bit mode and 4×4 bytes in the 128-bit mode, in which Aralash becomes a single 4×4 diamatrix product modulo 256 (invertible for odd keys). Round keys are updated by kₑ ← (SurKalit(kₑ) ⊕ RCⱼ) ∨ 1, independently of the state; the round constants RCⱼ are the first 320 bytes of the fractional part of √2, and C₁, C₂ the first 64 bytes of the fractional part of √3 with the LSB set (nothing-up-my-sleeve constants, as in SHA-2 [@shs]). The number of rounds is a parameter R with absorption points 0, R − 2 and R − 1; we recommend R = 6. Table @T:alg gives one iteration F, and Table @T:tv lists test vectors.")
code=["1: kₑ ← kₑ ∨ 1;  holat ← SBZ(Bog‘lash(holat, B), B)","2: for j = 1 to R do","3:     holat ← Aralash(holat, kₑ);  holat ← Bog‘lash(holat, kₑ)","4:     holat ← SurHolat(holat);  kₑ ← (SurKalit(kₑ) ⊕ RCⱼ) ∨ 1","5:     if j = R − 2 then holat ← SBZ(Bog‘lash(holat, B), B) else holat ← Bog‘lash(holat, kₑ)","6:     if j = R − 1 then holat ← SBZ(Bog‘lash(holat, B), B)","7: end for;  return (holat, kₑ)"]
T([9360],[["One iteration F(holat, B, kₑ) of the redesign"],["\n".join(code)]],"Pseudocode of the redesigned iteration","alg",mono=(0,))
T([2600,6760],[["Variant (Annex A key and message)","Hash value"],["Standard, 256-bit","55394f2d84fccd4d8d01d8fef756cd657ebecc3f96f81e5207767447de66e2b6"],["Standard, 128-bit","e9eaccdd4ccce2d229228c864989eb9f"],["Redesign, 256-bit, R = 6",KAT['32_6']],["Redesign, 128-bit, R = 6",KAT['16_6']],["Redesign, 256-bit, R = 10",KAT['32_10']],["Redesign, 128-bit, R = 10",KAT['16_10']]],"Test vectors","tv",mono=(1,))

H1('VII. Security Evaluation of the Redesign')
H2('A. Differential Bound')
P("We count active S-boxes in the byte-level truncated difference model in the single-key setting (no difference in kₑ), giving the adversary every cancellation that is possible. In a half-step with k active bytes in U: if k = 1 the modular sum J necessarily changes, so the S-boxes computing aᵢ are active in at least m − 1 positions; if k ≥ 2 the adversary may choose ΔJ = 0 and ΔK = 0, and then exactly k S-boxes for aᵢ and k for bᵢ are active. An element whose parameters do not change has an active output S-box exactly when it is active; an element whose parameters change may become active or not, at the adversary's choice.")
P("*Theorem 1.* Every differential characteristic with a non-zero state difference has at least eight active S-boxes in each Bog‘lash call.")
P("*Proof sketch.* Let s(0) = 0, s(1) = m and s(k) = 2k for k ≥ 2 be the minimal number of parameter S-boxes of a half-step whose source half has k active bytes. A dynamic program over the three half-steps, in which the adversary freely chooses the activity of every element with changed parameters, yields a minimum of 8 for m = 8 and m = 16; for m = 8 we confirmed the value by exhaustive search over all 2¹⁶ input patterns and all choices. The minimum is attained with two active bytes in the right half: two output S-boxes in the first half-step, four parameter S-boxes in the second and two output S-boxes in the third. ∎")
T([2600,2240,2240,2280],[["Half-step","Active input bytes","Active S-boxes","Active output bytes"],["1: right ← f(right; left)","left: 0; right: 2","2 (output)","right: 2"],["2: left ← f(left; right)","U = right: 2 (ΔJ = ΔK = 0)","2 (aᵢ) + 2 (bᵢ)","left: 0 (cancelled)"],["3: right ← f(right; left)","left: 0; right: 2","2 (output)","right: 2"],["Total","","8 (p ≤ 2⁻⁴⁸)",""]],"A characteristic attaining the minimum of Theorem 1","min8")
P("Because Aralash (with odd keys), Bog‘lash and SurHolat are bijections and the key schedule is independent of the state, a non-zero state difference never vanishes. Hence every round has at least 16 active S-boxes and every characteristic over one round has probability at most 2⁻⁹⁶; the bounds 2⁻¹²⁸ (256-bit) and 2⁻⁶⁴ (128-bit) are reached after two rounds and one round, respectively (Fig. @F:f2a). For collision characteristics that start with a message difference and try to cancel the state difference at each absorption point, a one-byte block difference activates at least three parameter S-boxes in Bog‘lash(holat, B) and at least two S-boxes in SBZ, i.e., at least five per absorption point; three points per block and three more for the sum block NY give at least 30 active S-boxes and probability at most 2⁻¹⁸⁰, independently of R.")
H2('B. Linear Bound')
P("In a linear trail the adversary can route a mask through a Bog‘lash element without activating the parameter S-boxes, because the LSB of the Möbius map does not depend on the parameters. A masked byte of the right half then costs two active S-boxes per call and a byte of the left half one. Between calls the mask is permuted by SurHolat and spread by Aralash on the LSB layer through Mᵀ, which has branch number 6 for odd keys. Searching all initial masks of weight at most two (three in the 128-bit mode), we obtained the minimal numbers of active S-boxes in Table @T:bnd; with correlation at most 2⁻³ per S-box, the bounds 2⁻⁶⁴ and 2⁻³² are reached after three rounds. For fixed parameters the element map has maximal correlation up to 2⁻¹·⁷ (differential probability up to 2⁻⁴·²), but averaged over the parameters, which are S-box outputs of the other half, both drop to about 2⁻⁶; the bounds above rely only on S-box counting and are independent of this effect.")
c2=CH['lin_256']; c1=CH['lin_128_yagona']
T([2600]+[1126]*6,[["Rounds","1","2","3","4","5","6"],["Differential, both modes (≥)","16","32","48","64","80","96"],["Linear, 256-bit (≥)"]+[str(c2[str(r)]) for r in range(1,7)],["Linear, 128-bit (≥)"]+[str(c1[str(r)]) for r in range(1,7)]],"Minimal numbers of active S-boxes of the redesign","bnd")
IMG('fig2.png',16.5,6.1,"(a) Bounds on trails of the redesign as a function of the number of rounds; (b) throughput of the standard and of the redesign for different R.","f2")
FK['f2a']=FK['f2']+'a'; FK['f2b']=FK['f2']+'b'
H2('C. Algebraic and Integral Properties')
P(f"Every Bog‘lash element contains three S-boxes; after one call all 256 output bits have degree 7 or 8 with respect to any input byte, and cube sums on 16 message bits are at the level of a random function already after the first absorption. The affine model of the LSB layer used against the standard predicts the output with accuracy 0.50 (random guessing). An active byte of the state loses all integral properties, including LSB balance, within the first Bog‘lash call; over 20 trials the number of balanced LSBs per round was {min(EX['int_new_mean'])}–{max(EX['int_new_mean'])} of 32, as for a random function (Fig. @F:int). No integral distinguisher on the full hash was found in either mode, also with sets of 2¹⁶ messages.")
H2('D. Statistical Tests')
rows=[["Mode, R","Output bits flipped: mean / min / max","Bit flip probability range","LSB-layer rank","Affine model accuracy","Balanced output bits"]]
for bl in (32,16):
    for R in (4,6,10):
        r=YR[f'{bl}_{R}']; rows.append([f"{'256' if bl==32 else '128'}-bit, R = {R}",f"{r['HD'][0]} / {r['HD'][1]} / {r['HD'][2]}",f"{r['bit'][0]}–{r['bit'][1]}",str(r['rang']),fz(r['affin'],3),str(r['integral'])])
T([1600,2400,1500,1200,1400,1260],rows,"Statistical tests of the redesign (300 message pairs per test; ideal flips: 128 and 64)","stat")
P(f"Table @T:stat shows that all tests are at the level of a random function from R = 3 on; for comparison, the standard produces output ranks 23–30 with constant bits in the 256-bit mode and an affine LSB layer (accuracy 1.00) with 32 balanced bits in the 128-bit mode. The one-round avalanche matrix (Fig. @F:sac) has mean {fz(EX['sac_new'][0],3)} and no zero entries, compared with mean {fz(EX['sac_std'][0],3)} and {fz(100*EX['sac_std'][1],1)}% zero entries for the standard round. Hence the choice of R is driven by the bounds: three rounds suffice for the linear bound, and R = 6 provides a factor-two margin.")
T([2600,2253,2253,2254],[["Property","Standard","Recommendations 1–4 (Qo‘sh kept)","Redesign (R = 6)"],["Non-absorbing rounds: LSB relation","c = 1","c = 1","c ≈ 0"],["MSB trail over 7 rounds","0.34","0.38 (no kernel collisions)","0"],["Output relations with c = 1","≈6","0","0"],["Internal collisions (kernel)","3.1%","0","0"],["Min. active S-boxes per round","0","0 (only at absorption)","16"],["128-bit LSB layer","affine (100%)","≈0.5","≈0.5"],["Relative throughput (256 / 128)","1 / 1","≈1 / ≈1","1.8–2.15 / 3.3–3.6"]],"Comparison of the standard, the four recommendations and the redesign","cmp")

H1('VIII. Performance')
P("We implemented the standard and the redesign in C (gcc 13.3, -O2) and measured single-core throughput on a 2.3 GHz Intel Xeon virtual machine for 256 KiB messages, taking the best of repeated runs. Because measurements on the virtual machine varied by up to 20%, Table @T:thr gives ranges over two full measurement series.")
T([3600,1920,1920,1920],[["Metric (one call)","Qo‘sh","Bog‘lash, 256-bit","Bog‘lash, 128-bit"],["Modular multiplications","706","192","96"],["Modular inversions (table)","192","48","24"],["S-box look-ups","0","144","72"],["Additions / XORs","≈1152","≈384","≈256"],["Data-dependent branches","132","0","0"],["Time (C), ns","242 / 234","133","80"]],"Cost of Qo‘sh and Bog‘lash","cost")
T([3600,2880,2880],[["Variant","256-bit mode","128-bit mode"],["Standard","1 (3.8–4.4 MB/s)","1 (1.7–2.2 MB/s)"],["Recommendations 1–4 (Qo‘sh kept)","≈1 (±2%)","≈1 (±2%)"],["Redesign, R = 10","1.1–1.6×","2.2–2.7×"],["Redesign, R = 6","1.8–2.15×","3.3–3.6×"],["Redesign, R = 4","2.6–3.3×","4.3–5.4×"]],"Throughput relative to the standard (C, -O2)","thr")
P("Qo‘sh accounts for about 91% of the operations of the standard (22 calls and 4224 modular inversions per block). Bog‘lash is 1.8–2.2 times faster per call in the 256-bit mode and 2.6–2.9 times in the 128-bit mode, where Qo‘sh still processes 64 elements (Table @T:cost). Recommendations 1, 2 and 4 alone change the throughput by less than 2%, the feed-forward costing 32 XORs per block and the output transformation two Bog‘lash calls per message (about 0.27 µs). With R = 6 the number of Bog‘lash calls per block drops from 22 to 14 (Fig. @F:f2b).")

H1('IX. Discussion and Limitations')
P("The differential bound of Theorem 1 holds for characteristics in the single-key setting under the usual independence assumptions; the effect of differentials (clustering of characteristics) and related-key differences in kₑ were not analysed. The linear bound is derived in an LSB-layer model from a search over low-weight initial masks rather than an exhaustive search; we consider it indicative and plan to confirm it with MILP-based tools [@mouha], [@zhao20]. Our attacks on the standard are experimental and their success rates were measured on random keys and messages; the practical attacks concern the 128-bit mode, while for the 256-bit mode we report distinguishers and reduced margins. Performance figures were obtained on a virtual machine with straightforward, non-vectorized code, so absolute numbers are conservative and only ratios should be compared. Finally, table look-ups of S-boxes require attention with respect to cache-timing and other side-channel attacks [@kocher], [@acikkapi19], as for any S-box based design; Bog‘lash removes the data-dependent branches of Qo‘sh, which is an improvement in this respect.")

H1('X. Conclusion')
P("We presented the first public cryptanalysis of the Uzbek hash function standard O‘zMSt 285:2024. All non-absorbing rounds are T-functions, which makes the LSB layer linear, MSB differences persistent and integral properties long-lived; together with a mixing matrix that is singular modulo 2 for almost all keys, this leads to practical attacks on the full 128-bit mode and reduced margins for the 256-bit mode. Our redesign—an S-box based absorption, odd round keys, the new bijective mixing step Bog‘lash, a state-independent key schedule, feed-forward with an output transformation and a unified byte-oriented 128-bit mode—has at least 16 active S-boxes per round, removes all identified weaknesses and, with six rounds, is 1.8–3.6 times faster than the standard. Future work includes MILP-based confirmation of the linear bound, related-key analysis, constant-time and hardware implementations, and a formal proposal for the next revision of the standard.")
H1('Acknowledgment')
P("[Funding and acknowledgments.] [AI-use disclosure, as required by IEEE: e.g., “A large language model was used to assist with code development, data analysis and language editing; all results were verified by the authors.”]")

